{"condition_compatibility":"partial","confidence":0.75,"content_digest":"ac97eef69c8ace64383b3cdd0b8ec241a5657590eead00f652b763abadde84e8","created_at":"2026-08-23T12:43:24+00:00","direction":"meta_to_principle","generation_trace":[{"attempts":1,"input_sha256":"17f406209a72e93b30605ab6f3e3e1d71c29b01666b11e7943cbd0d00638d526","input_tokens":5799,"latency_ms":26121,"model":"deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4-Flash","output_sha256":"89ec05257fb6e03f1d280e979005a9781f4eb0d43cd522b8c458ed7c5e797854","output_tokens":2417,"prompt_sha256":"a0e24249a3438877119c33b7ce1b688152e1189df5765ebd53fae91f7c321069","prompt_template":"meta-grounding-v1","provider":"siliconflow","repair_attempted":false,"schema_valid":true,"transport_attempts":1}],"link_id":"foundation-link:432e0c59a4227e5b3fab7a56","meta_principle_id":"meta:foundations:uncertainty-propagation","meta_principle_revision":1,"principle_id":"prn:socio-technical-systems:01M0QAAH7ZNWRP2CE2JSPM6JAF","principle_revision":1,"rationale":"The meta-principle requires that output uncertainty reflect uncertain inputs and their dependence through the model chain. The new principle applies this requirement to multiscale material models by decomposing integral uncertainty into unit-mechanism analyses and propagating unit uncertainties via an interaction measure, a specialized propagation strategy.","relation_type":"specializes","review_attestation":{"actor":"principia-owner","basis":"Explicit Submit reviewed changeset confirmation in local Admin","reviewed_at":"2026-08-23T15:24:41+00:00","source_review_status":"unassessed","trace_id":"sync:01M0QKJMJD3A13DCG7YJ3ACW1G"},"review_status":"reviewed","revision":1,"scale_compatibility":"partial","schema_version":"global-foundation-link-v1","status":"active","updated_at":"2026-08-23T12:43:24+00:00","variable_compatibility":"compatible"}
{"condition_compatibility":"partial","confidence":0.62,"content_digest":"b746d76387134f4b2375d3f1d5a1bfb8bba29dfa51c3f905787b41ac0f8595be","created_at":"2026-08-23T13:23:06+00:00","direction":"meta_to_principle","generation_trace":[{"attempts":1,"input_sha256":"2667e4f4014f4a7f01f9fcaa791de7959b612db5ef8771768ed8ad0ecc79a4c1","input_tokens":6066,"latency_ms":31497,"model":"deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4-Flash","output_sha256":"9543272c8614fc50ef438537c003f9dff77d2e5b4a0a196b577ab2277c4fa36c","output_tokens":2385,"prompt_sha256":"a0e24249a3438877119c33b7ce1b688152e1189df5765ebd53fae91f7c321069","prompt_template":"meta-grounding-v1","provider":"siliconflow","repair_attempted":false,"schema_valid":true,"transport_attempts":1}],"link_id":"foundation-link:b4c40c6da8caa294142023fc","meta_principle_id":"meta:ai-ml:bias-variance","meta_principle_revision":1,"principle_id":"prn:ai-ml:01M0QCK23K1X312GMTNGRKZM8A","principle_revision":1,"rationale":"Both are population-level decompositions of an expected loss/score into irreducible uncertainty, a bias-like reliability term, and a variance-like resolution term. The bias-variance decomposition is a well-established formal template for such decompositions, and the forecast-score decomposition mirrors its structure under a different loss and probabilistic setting.","relation_type":"analogous_to","review_attestation":{"actor":"principia-owner","basis":"Explicit Submit reviewed changeset confirmation in local Admin","reviewed_at":"2026-08-23T15:24:41+00:00","source_review_status":"unassessed","trace_id":"sync:01M0QKJMJD3A13DCG7YJ3ACW1G"},"review_status":"reviewed","revision":1,"scale_compatibility":"compatible","schema_version":"global-foundation-link-v1","status":"active","updated_at":"2026-08-23T13:23:06+00:00","variable_compatibility":"partial"}
{"condition_compatibility":"compatible","confidence":0.8,"content_digest":"9456e7042d3e4ade281d2736e876eabba20bed3337c75306c82577f62d0f1c70","created_at":"2026-08-22T03:50:54+00:00","direction":"meta_to_principle","generation_trace":[],"link_id":"foundation-link:f5f35558923aca607617118b","meta_principle_id":"meta:ai-ml:data-quality-ceiling","meta_principle_revision":1,"principle_id":"prn:general:01M0CK5XGCBBVRZSHF1SVGCTHG","principle_revision":1,"rationale":"The meta-principle says model performance is bounded by the information in the data. The claim that different deep-learning architectures yield similar AUROC around 0.65–0.66 for CKD progression is consistent with a data-imposed ceiling: when the cohort and features limit available signal, architecture choice has little effect on discrimination. Variables and scale align with the meta-principle's scope.","relation_type":"supports","review_attestation":{"actor":"principia-owner","basis":"Explicit Submit reviewed changeset confirmation in local Admin","reviewed_at":"2026-08-22T04:12:58+00:00","source_review_status":"unassessed","trace_id":"sync:01M0KTQZ6M68SKHPKARZR7EKFP"},"review_status":"reviewed","revision":1,"scale_compatibility":"compatible","schema_version":"global-foundation-link-v1","status":"active","updated_at":"2026-08-22T03:50:54+00:00","variable_compatibility":"compatible"}
